\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Sequential estimation bias is approximately constant across confounding levels $\rho $, confirming that omission of the contamination term, not its correlation with judge severity, drives the bias. Joint G-MFRM reduces the bias slope by ${\sim }$69\% at all $\rho $ levels. Synthetic data: $N = 50$, $I = 100$, $J = 8$.}}{4}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bias_vs_rho}{{3}{4}{Sequential estimation bias is approximately constant across confounding levels $\rho $, confirming that omission of the contamination term, not its correlation with judge severity, drives the bias. Joint G-MFRM reduces the bias slope by ${\sim }$69\% at all $\rho $ levels. Synthetic data: $N = 50$, $I = 100$, $J = 8$}{figure.caption.6}{}}
