\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Contamination diagnostics ($N = 8$ pilot, 9 judges). (a)~Contamination susceptibility $\hat  {c}_n$ (posterior mean $\pm $ 95\% CI), ordered by magnitude. Older models (GPT-3.5-Turbo, GPT-4-0613) show 4--6$\times $ higher susceptibility than recent models (GPT-4o). (b)~Ability bias absorbed by MFRM-only estimation: $\Delta \theta = \hat  {\theta }_{\mathrm  {MFRM}} - \hat  {\theta }_{\mathrm  {G\text  {-}MFRM}}$. The monotonic relationship (Spearman $\rho = 1.0$; $N = 8$, interpret with caution) confirms that contamination inflates MFRM ability estimates for high-$c_n$ models, as predicted by the OVB analysis (Section~\ref {sec:confounding}).}}{7}{figure.caption.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:contamination_and_bias}{{5}{7}{Contamination diagnostics ($N = 8$ pilot, 9 judges). (a)~Contamination susceptibility $\hat {c}_n$ (posterior mean $\pm $ 95\% CI), ordered by magnitude. Older models (GPT-3.5-Turbo, GPT-4-0613) show 4--6$\times $ higher susceptibility than recent models (GPT-4o). (b)~Ability bias absorbed by MFRM-only estimation: $\Delta \theta = \hat {\theta }_{\mathrm {MFRM}} - \hat {\theta }_{\mathrm {G\text {-}MFRM}}$. The monotonic relationship (Spearman $\rho = 1.0$; $N = 8$, interpret with caution) confirms that contamination inflates MFRM ability estimates for high-$c_n$ models, as predicted by the OVB analysis (Section~\ref {sec:confounding})}{figure.caption.11}{}}
